<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="ethene biosynthesis I (plants)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="ETHYL-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: ETHYLENE-CMPD Ethene| (also known as ethylene) is known in plants, fungi and bacteria. In plants, ethene is an important hormone that regulates plant growth and development. It is well-known as a fruit-ripening hormone and is biologically active in trace amounts. Ethene also plays a general role as a growth inhibitor in promoting leaf and flower senescence and abscission. All plants produce ethene from |FRAME: MET| (as described here). In addition, two alternative ethene biosynthetic pathways have been reported from microorganisms. Most microorganisms produce only trace amounts of ethene via the |FRAME: CPD-479| (KMBA) pathway |CITS: [Kazuhiro92]| (see |FRAME: PWY-6854|). In that route ethene is produced non-enzymatically by hydroxyl radicals that are produced from molecular oxygen by an |FRAME: MONOMER-16774| |CITS: [3541827][2792734]|. In the second microbial pathway, which is found mostly in members of the |FRAME: TAX-136849|, ethene is formed enzymatically from |FRAME: 2-KETOGLUTARATE| (see |FRAME: PWY-6853|). Being the simplest unsaturated organic molecule, ethene is the building block for synthetic polymers including plastics such as polyethylene. Large amounts of ethylene are currently produced in a chemical process from fossil fuels. It is the organic compound that has the highest annual global production. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; In plants, methionine is the only known precursor of ethene biosynthesis. Ethene is synthesized from |FRAME: MET| through intermediates |FRAME: S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE| (SAM) and |FRAME: CPD-68| (ACC). Ethene synthesis can be induced by environmental stress and other plant growth hormones such as auxin. Its production and action can be inhibited by specific ethene production inhibitors such as silver and high concentration of CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;. The cloning and analysis of |FRAME: EC-4.4.1.14 &quot;1-aminocyclopropane-1-carboxylate synthase&quot;| (ACC synthase, EC 4.4.1.14) genes from a number of plants show that they form a multigene family that are differentially regulated. The transcripts accumulate as a response to a variety of triggers such as ethene, hormones, wounding, ripening, senescence and pollination |CITS: [10996253]|.</Description>
